f 1078 #edb/ref 17592186312737 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314683 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f825e814-46db-5a49-9ae3-db2a2ad9a646" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314987 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314089 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314987 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314251 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314987 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314465 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314987 #edb/ref 1069 "q-340647" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314987 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314987 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $F(x)={x}^{2}$ for $x\\in R,$ and let $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of $F$ from $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ to $x\\in R.$ The Fourier series of $f$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the expression for the coefficient ${b}_{2}$ below, where $⟨\\,,⟩$ denotes the usual inner product on functions over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{2} & ={{field-1}} \\\\  & ={{field-2}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314987 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0dea1a3b-78f2-5704-9965-e3fb9de63bfa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314987 #edb/ref 1074 "The usual inner product of functions $f$ and $g$ over $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ is given by\n\n$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFor a $2\\pi $-periodic function $f(x)$ that is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ),$ the Fourier series of $f$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{0},$ ${a}_{n},$ and ${b}_{n}$ are constants to be determined. \n\nNow, we find the inner product of $f(x)$ with $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2},\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩ & = \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the functions\n$1,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),…$\nare orthogonal with respect to the defined inner product, the only nonzero term in the final expression above is\n\n$$\n{b}_{2}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)]}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\pi -(-\\pi ))-\\frac{1}{8}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\pi )-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-4\\pi ))) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩ & ={b}_{2}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩ \\\\ {b}_{2} & =\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)⟩} \\\\ {b}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot {\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314991 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314269 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314991 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314451 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314991 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314683 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314991 #edb/ref 1069 "q-340662" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314991 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314991 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $F(x)={x}^{2}-6x$ for $x\\in R,$ and let $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of $F$ from $x\\in [0,2\\pi )$ to $x\\in R.$ The Fourier series of $f$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the expression for the coefficient ${b}_{4}$ below, where $⟨\\,,⟩$ denotes the usual inner product on functions over $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{4} & ={{field-1}} \\\\  & ={{field-2}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314991 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dc60bc57-5b9c-5f88-9bc5-5ecffd1981e9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314991 #edb/ref 1074 "The usual inner product of functions $f$ and $g$ over $x\\in [0,2\\pi )$ is given by\n\n$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFor a $2\\pi $-periodic function $f(x)$ that's piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,2\\pi ),$ the Fourier series of $f$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{0},$ ${a}_{n},$ and ${b}_{n}$ are constants to be determined. \n\nNow, we find the inner product of $f(x)$ with $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2},\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩ & = \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the functions\n$1,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),…$\nare orthogonal with respect to the defined inner product, the only nonzero term in the final expression above is\n\n$$\n{b}_{4}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\pi -0)-\\frac{1}{16}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(16\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩ & ={b}_{4}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩ \\\\ {b}_{4} & =\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)⟩} \\\\ {b}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({x}^{2}-6x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315082 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314211 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315082 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315082 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314547 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315082 #edb/ref 1069 "q-341309" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315082 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315082 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $F(x)={x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}$ for $x\\in R,$ and let $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of $F$ from $x\\in [0,2\\pi )$ to $x\\in R.$ The Fourier series of $f$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the expression for the coefficient ${a}_{0}$ below, where $⟨\\,,⟩$ denotes the usual inner product on functions over $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & ={{field-1}} \\\\  & ={{field-2}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315082 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a5448f9e-c9df-59b5-abdc-33e558c56456" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315082 #edb/ref 1074 "The usual inner product of functions $f$ and $g$ over $x\\in [0,2\\pi )$ is given by\n\n$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFor a $2\\pi $-periodic function $f(x)$ that's piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,2\\pi ),$ the Fourier series of $f$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{0},$ ${a}_{n},$ and ${b}_{n}$ are constants to be determined. \n\nNow, we find the inner product of $f(x)$ with $1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),1⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),1⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2},1⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),1⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),1⟩ & = \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,1⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),1⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the functions\n$1,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),…$\nare orthogonal with respect to the defined inner product, the only nonzero term in the final expression above is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,1⟩.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨1,1⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[x]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),1⟩ & =\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,1⟩ \\\\ {a}_{0} & =\\frac{2⟨f(x),1⟩}{⟨1,1⟩} \\\\ {a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525200 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186526109 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525200 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186526126 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525200 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186526130 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525200 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186526142 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525200 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186526146 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525200 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525200 #edb/ref 1045 "Introduction to Fourier Series" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525200 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186526109 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525200 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046644 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525200 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049306 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525200 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "5a7d7bdb-8d3b-55d5-bde7-c86a8c028831" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525231 #edb/ref 1080 "-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525231 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525231 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0c5912f1-7503-5114-bd94-c09318d2aa64" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525349 #edb/ref 1080 "are not orthogonal" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525349 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525349 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3b761040-6bc5-5857-9c79-db2a13d27611" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525695 #edb/ref 1080 "\\pi " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525695 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] 